    <section class="prose prose-lg mt-16 max-w-3xl">
      <h2>Safety for grown-up beginners</h2>
      <p>A kitchen table works fine for kit building if you keep a few habits.</p><ul><li><strong>Ventilate.</strong> Open a window or run a fume extractor so flux smoke is pulled away from your face. Our <a href="/guides/soldering-fume-safety/">soldering fume safety guide</a> explains what is in it.</li><li><strong>Respect the lead.</strong> With leaded solder, keep food and drinks off the bench and wash your hands when you finish. Lead-free solder avoids the issue but needs more heat.</li><li><strong>Use the stand every time.</strong> Put the iron back in its stand whenever you let go, even for a few seconds.</li><li><strong>Don't max out the heat.</strong> More heat is not better and can damage parts. Our <a href="/guides/soldering-iron-temperature-guide/">soldering iron temperature guide</a> has a settings chart.</li></ul><p>Joint looks dull or lumpy? Our guide to <a href="/guides/common-soldering-mistakes/">common soldering mistakes</a> shows how to spot and fix it, and <a href="/guides/how-to-fix-a-cold-solder-joint/">how to fix a cold solder joint</a> walks through reflowing one. If a part went in the wrong way round, see <a href="/guides/how-to-desolder/">how to desolder</a>. A meter from our <a href="/best-multimeters-for-electronics/">multimeters for electronics</a> page lets you trace faults with a continuity test.</p>
